<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Anglo-Mysore War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1767">1767</a>–<a href="/years/1769">1769</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> War (1767–1769) was fought between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a>'s Mysore and the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-of-hyderabad">Nizam of Hyderabad</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> drawn in at different stages. After Hyder's cavalry raided close to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a>, the Company accepted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-madras">Treaty of Madras</a> of 1769, which restored conquests on both sides and promised mutual aid. The war established Mysore as the leading rival to British power in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-anglo-mysore-war.jpg" alt="First Anglo-Mysore War" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Miljoshi · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anglo-Mysore_War_1_and_2.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> in 1707 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> fractured, and by the Third Carnatic War the British held footholds at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>. Mysore, nominally ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadiyar-dynasty">Wodeyar dynasty</a>, had passed in 1761 to the Muslim commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a>. Seeking a land link between Madras and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> obtained a decree from the Mughal emperor Shah Alam II in August 1765 granting it the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-circars">Northern Circars</a>. The Nizam, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-ali-khan">Asaf Jah II</a>, objected but could not afford war, and in November 1766 signed a treaty under which the Company lent him troops.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The war began in January 1767 when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> invaded northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>; Hyder paid them 30 lakh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a> to withdraw. The Nizam then advanced to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengaluru">Bangalore</a> with Company troops under Colonel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-smith">Joseph Smith</a>, but secretly allied with Hyder. Their combined army attacked a Company post at Changama, and Hyder was decisively repulsed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiruvannamalai">Tiruvannamalai</a> on 26 September 1767. He besieged Ambur in November until a relief column arrived. Weakened by these failures and by British advances in the Circars, the alliance split, and the Nizam withdrew and later made a new treaty with the Company.</p><p>In February 1768 a force from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangalore">Mangalore</a> after Hyder's small fleet deserted, but Hyder and his son Tipu retook it and the other ports. Meanwhile the British recovered posts in the Carnatic and joined a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> force under Morari Rao. Hyder was repulsed at Ooscota on 22 August and near Mulabagilu in October, where a ruse by Colonel Brooks saved Colonel Wood's detachment, but he then routed Wood near Bagalur. In November 1768 he crossed back into the Carnatic, and at last made a forced march of 130 miles in three days with 6,000 cavalry to the gates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> nearly undefended, the Company agreed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-madras">Treaty of Madras</a> on 29 March 1769, though it refused the offensive alliance Hyder wanted. When Hyder went to war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> in 1770, the British declined to help him. In 1779 the Marathas, Hyder and the Nizam agreed on joint action against the British, which led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-mysore-war">Second Anglo-Mysore War</a> in 1780. The struggle ended only in 1799 with the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a> and the restoration of the Wodeyars as British clients.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “First Anglo-Mysore War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hyder conquered Bednur in 1763 and Malabar in 1766, and fought the <b>First Anglo-Mysore War</b> of 1767–1769, ending it on favourable terms in the Treaty of Madras after his cavalry appeared near the city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mysore-1399-1799">Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799) · Military</a></li></ul></section>
